---

<Layout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: dates.published,
    modifiedTime: dates.modified,
    author: author.name,
    section: "Brand Reviews",
    tags: [
      "puori protein powder",
      "puori pw1",
      "puori whey review",
      "puori protein"
    ],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{
    text: "See Our Top Clean Protein Picks",
    link: "/best-clean-protein-powder/",
  }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <ArticleHeader kicker="Brand Review" badge="Label-Checked" author={author} dates={dates} mobileAd={false}>
      <Fragment slot="title">Puori Protein Powder Review</Fragment>
      Puori PW1 is a short-label, grass-fed whey with public batch testing. It is also one of the most expensive grams of protein you can buy. Here is when that trade makes sense.
    </ArticleHeader>

    <TableOfContents>
      <a href="#verdict">The Short Verdict</a>
      <a href="#who-is-puori">Who Puori Is</a>
      <a href="#label">What Is in PW1</a>
      <a href="#top-picks">The Range, Ranked</a>
      <a href="#testing">The Testing Claim</a>
      <a href="#cost">Cost per 25 g</a>
      <a href="#which-one">Which PW1 to Buy</a>
      <a href="#vs">Puori vs Alternatives</a>
      <a href="#skip">Who Should Skip It</a>
      <a href="#using">Getting the Most From It</a>
      <a href="#mistakes">Common Mistakes</a>
      <a href="#faq">FAQ</a>
    </TableOfContents>

    <!-- Content -->
    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 pb-16 lg:pb-24">
      <div class="prose prose-lg prose-blue max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="lede">
          Puori PW1 is a good protein powder with a genuinely unusual selling point: you can look up the contaminant results for the batch in your hands. What it is not is a bargain, or a big dose. At roughly 20 to 21 g of protein per flavored scoop and a price that lands near the top of the market, it makes sense for buyers who value a four-ingredient label and published testing above cost per gram, and very little sense for anyone else.
        </p>

        <QuickPicks products={PRODUCTS} />

        <div id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. The Short Verdict: Good Whey, Premium Price
          </h2>

          <p>
            Puori PW1 is a whey protein concentrate from grass-fed, pasture-raised cows, sweetened in its flavored versions with coconut sugar rather than sucralose or stevia. The flavored tubs carry four or five ingredients in total, and the brand says every batch is tested by an outside lab and certified through the Clean Label Project, with results you can check by scanning a QR code on the bag. That combination is rare. Most clean-label brands ask you to trust them; Puori publishes the receipts.
          </p>

          <p>
            The catch is dose and price. Retailer listings put the flavored PW1 at about 20 to 21 g of protein per roughly 30 g scoop, and one European label we found lists about 118 calories, 5 g of carbohydrate and just under 5 g of sugar for the vanilla. That is a respectable protein share, but it is not the 25 g you get from most isolates, and the bag holds about 30 servings for a price that typically runs in the high sixties to high seventies of dollars. Per 25 g of protein, that puts Puori among the most expensive whey powders we track.
          </p>

          <p>
            So is it any good? Yes, on its own terms. If you want a whey with no artificial sweeteners, no gums doing heavy lifting and a public contaminant record, PW1 is one of very few products that ticks every box at once. If you want the most protein per dollar, or a banned-substance certification for drug-tested sport, it is the wrong tub, and we point you to better options further down.
          </p>

          <p>
            Our label-based verdict: buy the Unflavored version if you blend your shakes and want the highest protein per scoop in the range, buy Bourbon Vanilla if you drink it in water or milk and want the flavor buyers mention most often, and skip Puori entirely if cost per gram is your first filter. Everything on this page is built from labels, retailer listings and the brand's published claims. We did not taste or lab-test the product ourselves.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">THE ONE-LINE ANSWER</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">A clean, verifiable whey that charges for the privilege.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Puori PW1 earns its place on our clean protein shortlist for its short label and public batch results. It loses points on protein per scoop and on cost per 25 g, so it is a choice for priorities, not for budgets.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="who-is-puori" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            2. Who Puori Is and What It Actually Sells
          </h2>

          <p>
            Puori is a small supplement brand built around a clean-label pitch: few ingredients, sourcing it is willing to name, and third-party testing it publishes rather than summarises. Retailer brand profiles describe it as founded in 2009, and business databases list Oliver Amdrup as founder and chief executive. Its Amazon listings are sold by Puori Inc. and shipped by Amazon, which matters because counterfeit risk is lower when the brand itself is the seller.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein range is narrow. PW1 is the whey, and for years it came in two flavors, Bourbon Vanilla and Dark Chocolate. In August 2026 the brand announced PW1 Unflavored, a single-ingredient whey protein concentrate that it says comes from pasture-raised, grass-fed cows in New Zealand. Retail listings for the flavored versions describe the milk as coming from grass-fed herds and, in some European markets, from Nordic farms, so the sourcing language varies by product and market. One US retailer marks the flavored PW1 as not made in the USA.
          </p>

          <p>
            Puori sells other supplements and bundles too, but for this review the only products that matter are the three PW1 variants. There is no Puori plant protein, casein or isolate we could find, which is worth knowing before you go looking for one. If you need a dairy-free shake, this brand cannot help you, and our <a href="/best-plant-based-protein-powder/">plant-based protein guide</a> is the better starting point.
          </p>

          <p>
            The brand sells direct and through retailers such as Rogue Fitness, plus a range of wellness shops in Europe. On Amazon US, the Bourbon Vanilla 30-serving bag has a long-running listing sold by Puori Inc.; other flavors and sizes come and go, and price trackers have shown some of them as out of stock at times. If the flavor you want is unavailable on Amazon, check the brand site or a large fitness retailer before paying a third-party reseller premium.
          </p>

          <p>
            Put simply, Puori is a one-product protein brand with a strong testing story. That focus is a strength when you want a clear answer, and a weakness when you want choice: there is no isolate for lactose-sensitive buyers, no bulk size for heavy users and no low-cost option.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="label" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            3. What Is Actually in PW1: The Label, Line by Line
          </h2>

          <p>
            The flavored PW1 ingredient list is short enough to read in one breath. For Bourbon Vanilla, retailers list whey protein concentrate from milk, organic coconut sugar, natural flavoring, vanilla powder and vanilla seeds. Dark Chocolate swaps the vanilla for organic cocoa powder alongside the whey, coconut sugar and natural flavor. There is no sucralose, no acesulfame potassium, no stevia, no added gums listed for the flavored bags and no proprietary blend. Puori markets the flavored line as free of artificial sweeteners, colorants and additives.
          </p>

          <p>
            The protein figure is where you need to pay attention. Rogue Fitness lists 20 g of protein per serving for the flavored PW1, while Puori's own retail copy and several overseas listings say 21 g. The Swedish label we found for Bourbon Vanilla gives 21 g of protein, about 118 calories, about 5.2 g of carbohydrate including about 4.8 g of sugar, and about 1.9 g of fat per 30 g scoop. Treat these as approximate and check the panel on your bag, because labels differ between markets and over time.
          </p>

          <p>
            Sugar is the trade-off for a clean sweetener. Nearly 5 g of sugar per scoop is not much in a balanced diet, but it is several grams more than you get from an isolate sweetened with sucralose or stevia. If you are counting every gram of carbohydrate on a cut, or managing blood sugar, that number matters more than the marketing does. The Unflavored version removes the sugar entirely, which is one reason we rank it highly for blenders.
          </p>

          <p>
            PW1 Unflavored is the cleanest label in the range: whey protein concentrate and nothing else, per the brand. Puori's launch release lists 24 g of protein, 12 g of essential amino acids, 6 g of branched-chain amino acids and 2.7 g of leucine per serving. That leucine figure clears the roughly 2.5 g threshold often cited for stimulating muscle protein synthesis, which the flavored scoops may not quite reach at 20 to 21 g of protein.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
            <div class="overflow-x-auto">
              <table class="w-full text-left">
                <thead class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-200">
                  <tr>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Variant</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Protein / scoop</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Sweetener</th>
                    <th class="px-4 py-4 font-bold text-gray-600 text-sm">Notable</th>
                  </tr>
                </thead>
                <tbody>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Bourbon Vanilla</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20-21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic coconut sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 118 kcal, about 4.8 g sugar (EU label)</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-gray-50 border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Dark Chocolate</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 20-21 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic coconut sugar</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Organic cocoa; check panel for calories</td>
                  </tr>
                  <tr class="bg-white border-b border-gray-100">
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 font-black text-gray-900">Unflavored</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">About 24 g</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">None</td>
                    <td class="px-4 py-4 text-gray-700">Single ingredient; 2.7 g leucine per brand</td>
                  </tr>
                </tbody>
              </table>
            </div>
          </div>

          <p>
            Because PW1 is a concentrate rather than an isolate, it carries more lactose and a little more fat than a filtered isolate. Most people tolerate whey concentrate well, but if dairy reliably upsets your stomach, read our explainer on <a href="/whey-vs-whey-isolate/">whey vs whey isolate</a> before you buy a premium bag you may not finish.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Picks -->
        <div id="top-picks" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            4. The Puori Range, Ranked
          </h2>

          <p>
            Puori's protein line is small: one whey, PW1, sold in Bourbon Vanilla, Dark Chocolate and a newer Unflavored version. We rank them by who they suit, then add two clearly labelled alternatives from other brands for buyers who want a sport certification or a lower cost per gram. Figures are per the label or retailer listings at the time of writing and vary by flavor.
          </p>

          {PRODUCTS.map((product, index) => (
            <ProductCard product={product} rank={index + 1} />
          ))}
        </div>

        <div id="testing" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            5. The Clean Label Project Claim, Explained
          </h2>

          <p>
            Puori's headline claim is that every batch is third-party tested and certified through the Clean Label Project, a nonprofit that screens consumer products for environmental and industrial contaminants. Puori says its testing covers more than 200 substances, including heavy metals, pesticides and BPA, and that buyers can scan a QR code on the bag or enter the batch number online to see the results for their own lot. That batch-level transparency is the main reason Puori appears in clean protein conversations at all.
          </p>

          <p>
            It helps to be precise about what that does and does not mean. Clean Label Project screening is about contaminants: lead, cadmium, arsenic, mercury, pesticide residues, plasticisers and similar. It is not a banned-substance program for drug-tested athletes. NSF Certified for Sport and Informed Sport test for prohibited stimulants, steroids and other substances on sport anti-doping lists. We found no NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport certification for PW1, so a tested athlete should not treat Puori's testing as a substitute.
          </p>

          <p>
            Puori also says its whey was named in the Clean Label Project's Clean 16 list of protein powders with the lowest contaminant levels, and that it received the nonprofit's Transparency Award. We could find these claims only in the brand's own press material, not on the Clean Label Project's list itself, so read them as brand claims. The QR-linked batch results are the part you can check for yourself, and they are more useful than any award.
          </p>

          <p>
            Is contaminant testing worth paying for? It depends on how much protein powder you use and what you would otherwise buy. Dairy proteins such as whey tend to test lower for heavy metals than many plant proteins, so the marginal benefit of a tested whey over an untested one is smaller than it would be for, say, a rice protein. Our broader look at <a href="/is-protein-powder-safe/">whether protein powder is safe</a> covers the contaminant evidence in more detail.
          </p>

          <div class="bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-6 lg:p-8 my-8">
            <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-[#38BDF8] mb-3">WHAT THE TESTING COVERS</p>
            <p class="text-xl font-bold text-white mb-3">Contaminants, yes. Banned substances, no.</p>
            <p class="text-gray-300 leading-relaxed m-0">Puori's Clean Label Project testing screens for heavy metals, pesticides and similar contaminants. If you compete in a drug-tested sport, look for NSF Certified for Sport or Informed Sport on the exact product and flavor instead.</p>
          </div>
        </div>

        <div id="cost" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            6. Cost per 25 g of Protein: Where Puori Lands
          </h2>

          <p>
            Price is where Puori stops looking like an easy recommendation. The flavored PW1 bag holds about 900 g, listed as 30 servings, and typically sells for somewhere between the high sixties and the high seventies of dollars depending on the retailer. At 20 to 21 g of protein per scoop, that works out to roughly $2.70 to $3.30 per 25 g of protein, approximately, before any subscription discount. Prices change often, so check the live listing.
          </p>

          <p>
            To put that in context, many mainstream whey blends sell for around a third to a half of that per 25 g, and even premium grass-fed isolates often land well below $3. Puori is not the most expensive powder on the market, but it sits in the top tier, and you pay that premium for a smaller scoop. Someone using two scoops a day would spend a meaningful amount more per month than they would on a certified isolate.
          </p>

          <p>
            The Unflavored version helps a little on protein density, at about 24 g per serving, but one retailer lists it at the same high-seventies price point and we could not confirm its serving count, so we cannot give a reliable cost per 25 g. If the bag holds a similar number of servings to the flavored version, the cost per 25 g would come in slightly below the flavored range because each scoop delivers more protein. Check the servings line on the listing before you compare.
          </p>

          <div class="overflow-hidden border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl my-8">
